# shell: desktop
# PVL-045/PVL-048: a listing that changes repaints the rows that changed,
# not every row it holds.  The view has accumulated the damage since the
# repaint helper was written; nothing consumed it, so every frame painted
# the whole list.
wait 8
note four rows, and a first frame that has to paint all of them
expect-rows 4
draw-reset
note the LAST row is taken away behind the shell's back
remove /tmp/trk_script/c.txt
wait 40
expect-rows 3
note what the frame painted is the vacated strip, not the three rows that
note did not move
expect-drawn 1
note and the rows that were NOT painted are still on the screen: the list
note area is filled before the rows are drawn, so skipping a row must skip
note its ground too
expect-ink 60 118
note PVL-075: one row's file changes, so one row is painted.  Anything that
note learns about a single row -- the change notice, the value read, the
note icon probe -- says which row, rather than asking for a whole frame.
note Three frames carry it: the notice learns the size, the icon probe
note learns the picture a tick later, and the reading comes back after the
note notice invalidated it.  Each paints that row and no other.
draw-reset
write /tmp/trk_script/a.txt hello there
wait 40
expect-drawn 3
expect-drawn at-least 1
note and a frame with no damage to go on still paints the whole listing:
note skipping there would leave a window that never refreshes
draw-reset
click-row 0
wait 8
expect-drawn at-least 3
note and the window as a whole is what a full redraw would draw
expect-same-as-redraw
quit
